  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1223 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nThe upper low is centered around Sioux City late this morning. It \ncontinues to pull bands of light rain and low clouds into portions \nsoutheast of a line from Ortonville to Pierre. Farther north, \nhowever, sunshine is beginning to filter through with more breaks in \nthe cloud coverage. This system is expected to pull farther south \nand east this afternoon taking the precip and clouds with it. \n\nThe mid-level moisture hangs around Tuesday along with some \nshortwave activity. Southeast winds increase on return flow as sfc \nhigh pressure sits over the northern Great Lakes and a Rocky \nMountain low settles over Wyoming. By Tuesday afternoon, a 25 to 30 \nkt llj sets up over central SD. Winds at the sfc will gust 30 to 35 \nmph. Overnight, these features shift slightly east and upper ridging \nbuilds into the Plains. The southerly sfc flow and llj will bring \nchances for showers and thunderstorms into Wednesday morning. Then \nthe low pressure wave moves into South Dakota for Wednesday \nafternoon with an upper low moving into ND amplifying the precip \npotential. Temperatures are forecast to top out in the 60s and 70s \non Wednesday under that ridge even with the clouds and rain. \n\nPersistent south to southeast flow at the sfc and a fairly active \npattern of shortwaves and 700 mb to 500 mb lows will keep off and on \nshower chances in the forecast into the weekend. While an active and \nrather wet pattern is in place this week, nothing looks particularly \nhazardous at this time.",
